  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\n- Mainly Rain Late Thanksgiving, Mixing with Snow Early Friday\n\nNo major changes with regards to the late Thanksgiving Day/Friday \nstorm system with regards to our area. Amplifying long wave trough \nresponsible for this system is now extending from the Upper Midwest \nall of the way down into the Eastern Gulf. The associated sfc low is \njust offshore of Florida over the Western Atlantic. This negatively \ntilted upper trough will pull the sfc low and the warm and moist air \nahead of it up into the area starting late on Thanksgiving from \nSouth to North.\n\n850 mb temperatures are up around +5 to +7C as the precipitation \nmoves in, which easily supports all rain at that time. We are still \nexpecting the upper low and the coldest of the air aloft (around -\n6C) producing the snow over MO to eventually rotate into the \nsouthern portion of the forecast area. The coldest air is expected \nto moderate to around -2C or so as it moves over the southern \nportion of the forecast area. \n\nThis cooler air will bring the best potential of rain, mixing with \nor briefly changing to snow after 06z Fri until Friday morning. The \nair aloft, forecast sfc temps (mid 30s F), and road temps (around \n40F) all will support quite limited potential for impactful \nsnowfall/accumulations. That said, we can not rule out some light \naccumulations on the grassy areas across the SE portion of the area, \nand the higher elevations of Central Lower where the melting layer \nwill be more shallow. It also should fall at a low impact time \n(overnight/Friday morning) before melting quickly Friday afternoon \nas temps warm into the lower 40s.\n\n- Rain chances return Sunday\n\nA brief shortwave will move over Lower Michigan on Saturday morning, \nbut no impacts are expected with surface features well to our north \nin Canada. A robust shortwave on Sunday yields our next round of \nprecipitation with a low to our south in Ohio. With the surface low \nlocated behind the strongest PVA, current model guidance has the low \nonly deepening to around 1001 mb over Kentucky before weakening as \nit treks northeast over Ohio. This vertically stacked system will \nmove through quickly, with precipitation beginning Sunday morning \nand tapering out by the evening hours. \n\nTemperatures at the 850 mb level on Sunday are between -2 and -4 C, \nwith the column above freezing below 4000ft. Surface temperatures \nwill be in the 40s throughout the day, with forecast highs reaching \nthe mid 40s across the CWA. While not nearly as warm as the 850mb \ntemps for Thursday's rainfall, colder air in the mid-levels is still \nneeded for a stronger snow signal. If the upper-level trough and \nassociated cold air move farther south into our region than modeled, \nthere is still a possibility of some snowflakes. Current model \nguidance suggests only rainfall, with more modest QPF totaling \naround 0.25-0.5\".\n\nAfter the passage of the low on Sunday, zonal flow aloft and surface \nhigh pressure will keep the start of next week dry and mild.",
